From 83e5c0354e5c20379a52b78ada41878a5a153175 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: plotarmordev <299844489+plotarmordev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 05:11:08 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 11/18] nemotron: a stream drafts on the FP8 subset head while
 its last 16 context tokens are draft ids, else on the whole head

The draft vocabulary's head proposes only its 49,152 ids, so on text outside them a chain stops at the first uncovered
token. Each round a stream now takes its head from its context's tail: the subset while the last 16 tokens (the
prompt's, until 16 are emitted) are all draft ids, else the target's whole head, which stays resident, so nothing is
loaded twice. The choice is the stream's own (Rounds.vocab, mtp.Vocab) and goes with every launch, chain level and
captured graph; batched chains project and sample each stream with its own head, and shared rounds are priced at the
whole head's costs. cuda_costs.measure times the windows once and each head's levels between the same widths, and
caches each head's table under its own key, so each head's depth rule prices its rounds by its own level.

Against the same base with the subset head always on, paired on GB10s and starting on the whole head until 16 tokens
were emitted: Spanish, French, German, Italian and Japanese drafted 30.3%, 22.7%, 22.3%, 27.5% and 82.7% faster and
the mixed-language prompt 27.0%, against 3.3% slower counting to 1,000, 1.7% on prose, 0.8% on code and 0.2% on
English. Counting the prompt's tail starts a covered prompt on the subset: on one GB10, counting took 30 rounds in six
of seven fresh boots where it had taken 31 to 34, and ran 9.2% faster; no language lost a round and German gained one.
